\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Macroeconomic Theory II: Homework 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem*
Consider the following social planner's problem.
 She maximizes the expected utility
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}u(C_{t},N_{t})\right]
\]

\end_inset

by choosing a sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t},\ N_{c,t},\ N_{i,t},\ I_{c,t},\ \mbox{and}\ I_{i,t}$
\end_inset

, subject to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
N_{t} & = & N_{c,t}+N_{i,t}\\
C_{t} & = & A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{1-\alpha}\\
I_{t} & = & I_{c,t}+I_{i,t}=A_{i,t}K_{i,t}^{\alpha}N_{i,t}^{1-\alpha}\\
K_{c,t+1} & = & (1-\delta)K_{c,t}+I_{c,t}\\
K_{i,t+1} & = & (1-\delta)K_{i,t}+I_{i,t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $K_{c,0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{i,0}$
\end_inset

 are given.
 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 are exogenous productivity shocks.
 This is a two sector economy.
 There is a consumption goods sector and an investment goods sector, each
 of which has its own production function.
 Answer the following quesitons.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive the first-order conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assume that the momentary utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $u(C_{t},N_{t})=\log C_{t}-\frac{\eta}{1+\eta}N_{t}^{\frac{\eta+1}{\eta}}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta>0$
\end_inset

.
 Notice that this is a particular form of the so-called King-Plosser-Rebelo
 utility.
 Rewrite the first order conditions using this specific utility funciton.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive the optimality condition that equates the marginal rate of substitution
 to the marginal product of labor, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\frac{\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial N_{c,t}}\right)}{\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial C_{t}}\right)}=\left(\frac{\partial C_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 cancels out from the condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Prove that in this model economy, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 cannot move in the same direction, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{c,t}\equiv N_{c,t}-N_{c,t-1}>0$
\end_inset

 is always accompanied by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{i,t}\equiv N_{i,t}-N_{i,t-1}<0$
\end_inset

 and vice versa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Since employment figures in different sectors largely comove in the real
 world, this model prediciton is clearly counterfactual.
 However, we may be able to fix this problem by adopting a different utility
 function.
 Remember that the general form of the King-Plosser-Rebelo utility function
 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(C_{t},N_{t})=\frac{\left[C_{t}v(1-N_{t})\right]^{1-\sigma}-1}{1-\sigma}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma>0$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is a positive, concave function: i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $v(\cdot)>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{\prime}(\cdot)>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{\prime\prime}(\cdot)<0$
\end_inset

.
 Do you think 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 can move in the same direction if we choose an appropriate form of KPR
 utility? If your answer is yes, explain what form will help comovement,
 and under what circumstance 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 can move in the same direction.
 If your answer is no, give supporting argument why you reach such a conclusion.
 (HINT: Derive the first order conditions using the general form of KPR
 utility.
 Observe similarities and differences.) 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, let us set up the Lagrangian to the constrained maximization problem
 above:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & E_{0}[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\{u(C_{t},N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})+\lambda_{t}(A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{1-\alpha}-C_{t})\\
 &  & +\mu_{t}(A_{i,t}K_{i,t}^{\alpha}N_{i,t}^{1-\alpha}-K_{c,t+1}+(1-\delta)K_{c,t}-K_{i,t+1}+(1-\delta)K_{i,t})\}]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

After taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

, we then obtain the first order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\{C_{t}\}:\ u_{c}(t)-\lambda_{t} & = & 0\nonumber \\
u_{c}(t) & = & \lambda_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

After taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

, we then obtain the first order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\{N_{c,t}\}:\ u_{N_{c}}(t)+\lambda_{t}(1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}} & = & 0\nonumber \\
-\frac{u_{N_{c}}(t)}{u_{c}(t)} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

After taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

, we then obtain the first order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\{N_{i,t}\}:\ u_{N_{i}}(t)+\mu_{t}(1-\alpha)\frac{I_{c,t}+I_{i,t}}{N_{i,t}} & = & 0\nonumber \\
-u_{N_{i}}(t) & = & \mu_{t}(1-\alpha)\frac{I_{c,t}+I_{i,t}}{N_{i,t}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

After taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{c,t+1}$
\end_inset

, we then obtain the first order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\{K_{c,t+1}\}:\ -\mu_{t}+E_{t}\left[\beta\lambda_{t+1}\alpha\frac{C_{t+1}}{K_{c,t+1}}+\beta\mu_{t+1}(1-\delta)\right] & = & 0\nonumber \\
E_{t}\left[\beta\lambda_{t+1}\alpha\frac{C_{t+1}}{K_{c,t+1}}+\beta\mu_{t+1}(1-\delta)\right] & = & \mu_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

After taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{i,t+1}$
\end_inset

, we then obtain the first order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\{K_{i,t+1}\}:\ -\mu_{t}+E_{t}\left[\beta\mu_{t+1}\left(\alpha\frac{I_{c,t+1}+I_{i,t+1}}{K_{i,t+1}}+(1-\delta)\right)\right] & = & 0\nonumber \\
E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{\mu_{t+1}}{\mu_{t}}\left(\alpha\frac{I_{c,t+1}+I_{i,t+1}}{K_{i,t+1}}+(1-\delta)\right)\right] & = & 1
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

, we then obtain the first order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\{\lambda_{t}\}:\ A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{1-\alpha}-C_{t} & = & 0\\
A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{1-\alpha} & = & C_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

After taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{t}$
\end_inset

, we then obtain the first order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\{\mu_{t}\}:\ A_{i,t}K_{i,t}^{\alpha}N_{i,t}^{1-\alpha}-K_{c,t+1}+(1-\delta)K_{c,t}-K_{i,t+1}+(1-\delta)K_{i,t}) & = & 0\\
K_{c,t+1}-(1-\delta)K_{c,t}+K_{i,t+1}-(1-\delta)K_{i,t}) & = & A_{i,t}K_{i,t}^{\alpha}N_{i,t}^{1-\alpha}\\
I_{c,t}+I_{i,t} & = & A_{i,t}K_{i,t}^{\alpha}N_{i,t}^{1-\alpha}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Plugging the constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=N_{c,t}+N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 into the given King-Plosser Rebelo utility function, we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(C_{t},N_{t})=u(C_{t},N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})=\log C_{t}-\frac{\eta}{1+\eta}(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{\eta+1}{\eta}}
\]

\end_inset

Now, we obtain the necessary partial derivatives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
u_{c}(t) & = & \frac{1}{C_{t}}\\
u_{N_{c}}(t) & = & -(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{1}{\eta}}\\
u_{N_{i}}(t) & = & -(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{1}{\eta}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Finally, we rewrite the first order conditions using the partial derivatives
 above:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{1}{C_{t}} & = & \lambda_{t}\\
(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{1}{\eta}} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{1}{N_{c,t}}\\
(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{1}{\eta}} & = & \mu_{t}(1-\alpha)\frac{I_{c,t}+I_{i,t}}{N_{i,t}}\\
E_{t}\left[\beta\alpha\frac{1}{K_{c,t+1}}+\beta\mu_{t+1}(1-\delta)\right] & = & \mu_{t}\\
E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{\mu_{t+1}}{\mu_{t}}\left(\alpha\frac{I_{c,t+1}+I_{i,t+1}}{K_{i,t+1}}+(1-\delta)\right)\right] & = & 1\\
A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{1-\alpha} & = & C_{t}\\
I_{c,t}+I_{i,t} & = & A_{i,t}K_{i,t}^{\alpha}N_{i,t}^{1-\alpha}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first order condition of the maximization problem with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{N_{c}}(t)+\lambda_{t}(1-\alpha)A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{-\alpha}=0
\]

\end_inset

By (6) and (2), we have that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{N_{c}}(t)+u_{c}(t)(1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}} & = & 0\\
-\frac{u_{N_{c}}(t)}{u_{c}(t)} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}\\
-\frac{\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial N_{c,t}}\right)}{\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial C_{t}}\right)} & = & \left(\frac{\partial C_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial C_{t}}{\partial N_{c,t}}=(1-\alpha)A_{c,t}K_{c,t}^{\alpha}N_{c,t}^{-\alpha}=(1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, by (11) and (12), we have that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-\frac{\left[-(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{1}{\eta}}\right]}{\left[\frac{1}{C_{t}}\right]} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}\nonumber \\
(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{1}{\eta}} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{1}{N_{c,t}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 cancels out from the condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From equation (14), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N_{c,t}(N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})^{\frac{1}{\eta}}=(1-\alpha)
\]

\end_inset

It is obvious from this optimal condition that 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 move in opposite directions.
 Since the right hand side of the condition is a constant, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{i,t}<0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{c,t}>0$
\end_inset

.
 If this were not the case, then the left hand side would vary over time,
 so the optimality condition would be violated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part e.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Plugging the constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=N_{c,t}+N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 into the general King-Plosser Rebelo utility function, we get:
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(C_{t},N_{t})=u(C_{t},N_{c,t}+N_{i,t})=\frac{\left[C_{t}v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})\right]^{1-\sigma}-1}{1-\sigma}
\]

\end_inset


\series default
First, we obtain the necessary partial derivatives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{c}(t) & = & [C_{t}v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})]^{-\sigma}v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})\\
u_{N_{c}}(t) & = & -[C_{t}v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})]^{-\sigma}C_{t}v^{\prime}(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})\\
u_{N_{i}}(t) & = & -[C_{t}v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})]^{-\sigma}C_{t}v^{\prime}(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Substituting the necessary partial dervivaties into (7) we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{C_{t}v^{\prime}(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})}{v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})} & = & (1-\alpha)\frac{C_{t}}{N_{c,t}}\nonumber \\
\frac{v^{\prime}(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})}{v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})} & = & \frac{(1-\alpha)}{N_{c,t}}\nonumber \\
v^{\prime}(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t}) & = & \frac{(1-\alpha)}{N_{c,t}}v(1-N_{c,t}-N_{i,t})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

After taking the derivative of expression (15) with respect to time, we
 obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})\left(-\frac{dN_{c,t}}{dt}-\frac{dN_{i,t}}{dt}\right)=\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}\left(-\frac{dN_{c,t}}{dt}-\frac{dN_{i,t}}{dt}\right)-\frac{(1-\alpha)v(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}^{2}}\frac{dN_{c,t}}{dt}\\
v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})(-\dot{N}_{c,t}-\dot{N}_{it})=\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}(-\dot{N}_{c,t}-\dot{N}_{it})-\frac{(1-\alpha)v(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}^{2}}\dot{N}_{c,t}\\
\left[v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})+\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}+\frac{(1-\alpha)v(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}^{2}}\right]\dot{N}_{c,t}=\left[v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})-\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}\right]\dot{N}_{it}\\
\frac{dN_{i,t}}{dt}=\frac{\left[v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})+\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}+\frac{(1-\alpha)v(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}^{2}}\right]}{\left[v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})-\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}\right]}\frac{dN_{c,t}}{dt}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v(\cdot)>0,v^{\prime}(\cdot)>0,v^{\prime\prime}(\cdot)<0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})+\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}+\frac{(1-\alpha)v(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}^{2}}\right]>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left[v^{\prime\prime}(1-N_{t})-\frac{(1-\alpha)v^{\prime}(1-N_{t})}{N_{c,t}}\right]<0$
\end_inset

.
 So, when
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{c,t}\approx\frac{dN_{c,t}}{dt}>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta N_{i,t}\approx\frac{dN_{i,t}}{dt}<0$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{i,t}$
\end_inset

 move in opposite direction using the general form of the KPR utility, then
 we will be unable to find an appropriate form of KPR utility in which they
 move in the same direction.
\end_layout

